UID:3b9654b0696d273a@role-civil-litigation-appellate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260919T050027Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260623
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Blanche v. Lau
DESCRIPTION:PRELIMINARY PRINT \nVolume 609 U. S. Part 1 \nPages 1–29 \nO
 FFICIAL REPORTS \nOF \nTHE SUPREME COURT \nJune 23\, 2026 \nREBECCA A. WOM
 ELDORF \nreporter of decisions \nPage Proof Pending Publication\nNOTICE: T
 his preliminary print is subject to formal revision before \nthe bound vol
 ume is published. Users are requested to notify the Reporter \nof Decision
 s\, Supreme Court of the United States\, Washington\, D. C. 20543\, \npio@
 supremecourt.gov\, of any typographical or other formal errors. \nCASES AD
 JUDGED \nIN THE \nSUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES \nAT \nOCTOBER TERM\,
  2025 \nBLANCHE\, ACTING ATTORNEY GENERAL v. \nMUK CHOI LAU \ncertiorari t
 o the united states court of appeals for \nthe second circuit \nNo. 25–4
 29. Argued April 22\, 2026—Decided June 23\, 2026 \nUnder the Immigratio
 n and Nationality Act (INA)\, the Government can \nremove aliens applying 
 for admission to the country if they are “ `inad-\nmissible\,' ” and i
 t can remove aliens already admitted if they are “ `de-\nportable.' ” 
 Campos-Chaves v. Garland\, 602 U. S. 447\, 451. In this \ncase\, responden
 t Muk Choi Lau\, a Chinese citizen\, was admitted to the \nUnited States a
 s a lawful permanent resident in 2007. On May 7\, 2012\, \nNew Jersey char
 ged Lau with trademark counterfeiting. While await-\ning trial\, Lau tempo
 rarily left the United States for China. On June \n15\, 2012\, Lau attempt
 ed to reenter the United States by presenting \nhimself to a border offcer
  at the airport. Lawful permanent residents \ngenerally must be regarded a
 s already admitted to the country and usu-\nally do not have to reapply fo
 r admission when they return from tempo-\nrary overseas travel. 8 U. S. C.
  § 1101(a)(13)(C). Under an exception\, \nthe Government may regard a law
 ful permanent resident as “seeking \nan admission” (and thus as not al
 ready admitted) if he “has committed \nan offense identifed in section 1
 182(a)(2)\,” § 1101(a)(13)(C)(v)\, including a \ncrime involving moral 
 turpitude\, § 1182(a)(2)(A)(i)(I). Because of Lau's \npending criminal ch
 arge\, the border offcer did not \nhttps://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/2
 5pdf/609us1r53_i4dk.pdf
URL:https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/609us1r53_i4dk.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
